A||||1||GarethWright||Seat Fabric Repair||||1||
Z||000000||GarethWright||08-25-2001||03:40 AM||||Hello, the seam in the back of the seat of my drivers side chair has split, I figure the best way to repair this is to pull the chair and take the fabric all the way off, so as to remove tension from the stitching, at least until I stick the fabric back on the padding, is this correct?.<BR>Also, while I have the fabric off, whats the best way to clean the fabric/padding? I'm assuming that the foam padding ISN'T machine washable  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">, Is it safe to put the seat fabric itself in the wash, or should I do if by hand?  If this isn't too tough, and turns out nicely, I will be cleaning the passengers side either sunday or next weekend.<BR>Either way, I don't want to put animal-print seat covers over otherwise really nice seats  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Thanks in advance.<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroBUZZ||08-25-2001||08:41 AM||||Gareth:<BR>If your fabric is is really good shape it's probably worth repairing.  When you remove the cover (hog rings, tension bars et al) the cover comes off as one piece, the fabric, foam padding and vinyl backing.  You will need a heavy duty sewing machine to restitch. <BR>Alternatively you can replace the damaged cover with a good one as they seem to be fairly widely available.<BR>I used a rented carpet cleaner w/ fabric wand to clean my seats.  They were out of the car but all assembled. Worked great.  It's hard to believe all the filth that came out of them.   Good luck<BR>Gary<BR>86 SE <BR>+<BR>various eyesore cars||||reg||6
